or=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4602\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4562\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4522\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5000\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5040\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5080\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5398\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5438\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5478\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502141 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c30b7d81-9bb5-5e5f-938c-eb285e73259b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502141 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=300,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{150\\,000}{500\\,000}=0.3.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=300(0.3)=90>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=300(1-0.3)=210>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=300$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,\\frac{0.3(1-0.3)}{300}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,0.0007).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If between $72$ and $90$ of users in the sample have a paid account, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{72}{300}\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le \\frac{90}{300} \\\\ 0.24\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le 0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.24\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.3).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.24\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.3) & =P(\\frac{0.24-0.3}{\\sqrt{0.0007}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.3-0.3}{\\sqrt{0.0007}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.27\\le Z\\le 0) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0)-P(Z\\le -2.27) \\\\  & =Φ(0)-Φ(-2.27)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0)=0.5000,\\,Φ(-2.27)=0.0116.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.24\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.3) & =Φ(0)-Φ(-2.27) \\\\  & =0.5000-0.0116 \\\\  & =0.4884.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502151 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502151 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146816" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502151 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502151 #edb/ref 1071 "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=100$ from a population of size $N=500$ in which $90%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=60$ from a population of size $N=1\\,500$ in which $90%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=40$ from a population of size $N=1\\,500$ in which $90%$ of the population has a particular characteristic." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502151 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d6efb51f-1cc6-56d3-90b2-77b3cfdb82a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502151 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =100, \\\\ 5%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 500 \\\\  & =25 \\\\  & <100.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  the sample size is more than $5%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =60 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,500 \\\\  & =75\\,✓ \\\\ np & =60(0.90) \\\\  & =54 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =60(1-0.90) \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,500 \\\\  & =75\\,✓ \\\\ np & =40(0.9) \\\\  & =36 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =40(1-0.9) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502154 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502154 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146972" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502154 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502154 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=75$ from a population where $45%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that less than $50%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502154 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d98ad49e-5d4d-5c1b-b380-745357c60a55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502154 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=75,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.45.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=75(0.45)=33.75>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=75(1-0.45)=41.25>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=75$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,\\frac{0.45(1-0.45)}{75}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,0.0033).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.5).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.5) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{0.5-0.45}{\\sqrt{0.0033}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le 0.87) \\\\  & =Φ(0.87)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502164 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502164 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147479" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502164 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502164 #edb/ref 1071 "A dried apricot producer knows that $70%$ of apricots weigh more than $40$ g. If the factory randomly samples $350$ apricots, find a normal approximation for the probability that fewer than $224$ apricots weigh more than $40$ g. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502164 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fff0efc8-e9b9-52d5-b287-771e1cbfdc28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502164 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=350,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.7.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=350(0.7)=245>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=350(1-0.7)=105>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=350$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{350}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,0.0006).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If fewer than $224$ apricots in the sample weigh more than $40$ g, then \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}<\\frac{224}{350}=0.64.\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.64).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.64) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.64-0.7}{\\sqrt{0.0006}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-2.45) \\\\  & =Φ(-2.45).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
